   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to designating Charles lake, Lost lake, Putnam lake and Tonetta lake as inland waterways for purposes of waterfront revitalization",
    "basePrintNo": "S6920",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6920-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §911, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6920\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the executive law, in relation to\ndesignating Charles lake, Lost lake, Putnam lake and Tonetta lake as\ninland waterways for purposes of waterfront revitalization\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nDesignates Charles Lake, Lost Lake in Putnam County, Putnam Lake and\nTonetta Lake as inland waterways for purposes of waterfront\nrevitalization.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends paragraph (a) of subdivision 4 of section 911 of\nthe executive law, regarding the definition of \"inland waterways,\" by\nadding the following lakes: Charles, Lost (in the county of Putnam),\nPutnam and Tonetta.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nBedrock aquifers underlie all of Putnam County, and face threats from\nmunicipal stormwater, sewer and septic systems, especially where these\nbedrock formations occupy common drainage areas that emerge in\nstreams. These bedrock formations support the wells which yield most\nof the drinking water for county residents.\n\nConcentrated residential areas lay around many of Putnam County's\nwaterbodies, including Peach Lake (added last year to the list of\ninland waterways), Lake Tonetta in Southeast and Putnam Lake in\nPatterson. This development poses threats not only to the recreational\nand ecological health of these waterways, but also to the myriad parts\nof the New York City Reservoir system formed by damming and impounding\nwaterways in Putnam County and the very aquifer system itself.\n\nThe Great Swamp, unique and beautiful, lies between the Housatonic and\nHudson River super-watersheds. It is one of the largest wetlands in\nNew York State. To the north, it traverses the towns of Pawling and\nDover via the Swamp River, then the Ten Mile River. It crosses into\nConnecticut, where it meets the Housatonic River and eventually flows\ninto Long Island Sound.\n\nThe south flow traverses the towns of Patterson and Southeast via the\nEast branch Croton River, then it flows into Westchester via the East\nBranch, then the East Branch Reservoir of New York City's Croton\nReservoir System, making the Great Swamp important headwaters for New\nYork City's water supply.\n\nOriginally intended as summer bedroom communities, Putnam Lake and\nTonetta Lake were formed by impounding as the New York City Reservoir\nsystem was created. Residents now live there year round and both\nremain popular tourist destinations. Both of these waterways are\npriority impaired and are also included in the Section 303(d) List of\nImpaired Waters. Putnam Lake further drains into the watershed through\nits outflow toward Lost Lake and Lake Charles. Further ecological harm\ncould threaten the Great Swamp Critical Environmental Area as well as\nthe Reservoir System.\n\nIt is imperative that these waterways be added to the list of inland\nwaterways so the towns of Southeast and Patterson can prepare local\nwaterfront revitalization plans (LWRP) to better manage the\nenvironmental threats these lakes face from increased development.\nThere is also economic development potential to be realized, Lake\nCharles was once to become the site of a business park with a movie\ntheater. The comprehensive management strategy that will be achieved\nthrough the LWRP planning process will be a boon to the program's\nstrategic goal of regional planning that will enable smarter, more\nsustainable growth for the East Branch Croton River/Diverting and East\nBranch Reservoir Watershed.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the state\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
